<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cetshwayo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1826–<a href="/years/1884">1884</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Cetshwayo kaMpande</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cetshwayo kaMpande was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> from 1872 to 1879. He won the succession by defeating his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbuyazi">Mbuyazi</a> at Ndondakusuka in 1856, rebuilt the regimental system and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulundi">Ulundi</a> his capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> invaded in 1879; after the Zulu victory at Isandlwana his army was broken at Ulundi, and he was captured and exiled. Partly restored in 1883, he was defeated by Zibhebhu and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshowe">Eshowe</a> in 1884.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cetshwayo.jpg" alt="Cetshwayo" width="320" height="359" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Bassano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cetshwayo_ka_Mpande,_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cetshwayo kaMpande was the son of the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpande">Mpande kaSenzangakhona</a> and Queen Ngqumbazi, and a half-nephew of King Shaka. In the Zulu Civil War of 1856 he led the Usuthu against his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbuyazi">Mbuyazi</a>, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ndondakusuka">Battle of Ndondakusuka</a> on 2 December 1856 he annihilated most of Mbuyazi's Gqoza followers, among them five of his own brothers. Mpande recognised him as heir in 1861. With the advice of the trader John Robert Dunn, Cetshwayo confined the import of firearms to the Usuthu, which gave him a technological edge and weakened the military power of rivals such as his half-brother uHamu kaNzibe and Zibhebhu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpande">Mpande</a> died in 1872, and his death was first concealed to ensure a smooth transition. Cetshwayo was acclaimed by his izinduna in 1873, and on 1 September 1873 Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophilus-shepstone">Theophilus Shepstone</a>, Natal's Secretary for Native Affairs, presided over his coronation. Rival chiefs, among them uHamu, Zibhebhu and Mnyamana kaNgqengelele, declared their loyalty, which centralised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> military power. Cetshwayo then founded a new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulundi">Ulundi</a>.</p><p> After the British annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>, a Boer-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> border dispute became a British-Zulu one, and a boundary commission of 1878 found that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> had no right to the land. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartle-frere">Sir Henry Bartle Frere</a> withheld its findings.</p><p>On 11 December 1878 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartle-frere">Bartle Frere</a> issued an ultimatum demanding the abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> military system within thirty days and payment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. It could not be met, and British forces entered Zululand on 11 January 1879. Cetshwayo opposed the war and sent messengers seeking peace, whom the British imprisoned. After the Zulu victory at Isandlwana he refused to counterattack into Natal, hoping for a treaty. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ulundi">Battle of Ulundi</a> on 4 July the British formed a hollow square with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gatling-gun">Gatling guns</a>, routed the Zulu after about 45 minutes and burnt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulundi">Ulundi</a>. Cetshwayo was captured on 28 August and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, and Zululand was divided into thirteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bishop Colenso and Lady Florence Dixie took up his cause, and in 1882 he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and was received by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>. The British returned him to Zululand in 1883, but Zibhebhu attacked his kraal at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulundi">Ulundi</a> on 22 July 1883 and wounded him. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshowe">Eshowe</a>, where he died on 8 February 1884, presumably of a heart attack, though some think he was poisoned. His son Dinuzulu was proclaimed king on 20 May 1884. Cetshwayo is remembered above all as the last independent king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cetshwayo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A succession war between Mpande’s sons ended in 1856 at the Battle of Ndondakusuka, where <b>Cetshwayo</b> defeated his brother Mbuyazi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cetshwayo</b>’s army destroyed a British force at Isandlwana in 1879, but after the defeat at Ulundi that July he was captured and exiled to Cape Town.</blockquote><a href="/societies/zulu-kingdom">Social Hierarchy of the Zulu Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1884 Boers led by Lucas Meyer, among them the young Louis Botha, backed Dinuzulu, son of <b>Cetshwayo</b>, against Zibhebhu, and in return claimed a huge tract of Zulu land, where they proclaimed the Nieuwe Republiek with Lucas Meyer as president.</blockquote><a href="/state/nieuwe-republiek">Nieuwe Republiek</a></li><li><blockquote>Dinuzulu, son of <b>Cetshwayo</b>, led resistance in 1888; he was tried for treason and exiled to St Helena.</blockquote><a href="/state/zululand-british-colony">Zululand (British colony)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshowe">Eshowe</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-28.89,31.45&amp;year=1863" aria-label="Eshowe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5371 3220 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Cetshwayo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5821 3490h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5821 3490h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=-28.89,31.45&amp;year=1863">What was here in 1863? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/mpande"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mpande.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Mpande</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; c. 1798–1872</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/dinuzulu-kacetshwayo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dinuzulu-kacetshwayo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Dinuzulu kaCetshwayo</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 1868–1913</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Why does the Governor of Natal speak to me about my laws? Do I go to Natal and dictate to him about his laws?&#8221;</p><footer>Message to the Governor of Natal, 1876 &#183; contemporary translation (interpreter not named), 1876 &#183; as printed in the British Blue Book and quoted by H. Rider Haggard (1882) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8667/pg8667-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Have I not asked the English to allow me to wash my spears since the death of my father ‘Umpandi,’ and they have kept playing with me all this time, treating me like a child?&#8221;</p><footer>Message to the Governor of Natal, 1876 &#183; contemporary translation (interpreter not named), 1876 &#183; as printed in the British Blue Book and quoted by H. Rider Haggard (1882) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8667/pg8667-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
